# Making Delivery Detours Understandable: A One-Screen Redesign of Food-Delivery Order Tracking

> A self-directed product case study. One problem, one screen, one measurable goal.

| | |
|---|---|
| **Type** | Product UX improvement (case study) |
| **Role** | Product Manager (self-directed) |
| **Scope** | One screen: live order tracking |
| **Status** | Concept and prototype stage. Not yet validated with users |

> **Disclaimer:** Independent case study. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Swiggy, Zomato, or any other company, and no internal data was used. Screens in `assets/` are my own recreations of a common tracking layout, with made-up names and places. Any real app screenshots I add are for commentary and have personal details blurred.

---

## 1. Summary

When a delivery partner takes an unexpected route, customers cannot tell whether another delivery stop is affecting their order. The tracking screen shows a map and an ETA, but not *why* the route looks the way it does.

**Proposal:** keep the familiar tracking layout and add three things:

1. An ETA that is more prominent and visibly flags changes.
2. A short delivery sequence explaining a *confirmed* nearby stop.
3. A one-line "Why this route?" explanation that never reveals other customers' details.

**Primary metric:** a 1 to 5 delivery-status clarity score.

---

## 2. Before and after

| Before: current layout | After: redesign |
|---|---|
| ![Before](assets/before.png) | ![After](assets/after.png) |

Both screens share the same structure: map, header, partner marker, home pin, status card, ETA badge, delivery-instructions row, and call button. The redesign only **adds** elements.

| # | Addition | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | ETA as a clock time, with an "Updated +N min" tag when it changes | Customers anchor on the ETA first. Visible changes build trust |
| 2 | Delivery sequence: picked up, then nearby drop-off, then your order | Answers "is another stop affecting my order?" at a glance |
| 3 | "Why this route?" line with a privacy note | Explains the detour in plain language |
| Map | "Quick stop" marker on the route. Solid line is the current leg and dashed line is the leg after the stop | Makes the route match what the card says |

---

## 3. Problem

Frequent customers may feel uncertain when their delivery partner travels along an unexpected route. Without a clear explanation of the delivery sequence, they may not know whether another stop is affecting their order.

- **Origin:** a personal customer observation.
- **Not yet known:** how often this happens, how many customers it affects, and whether batching is the real cause (see [Limitations](#9-limitations)).

## 4. Target user

Frequent food-delivery customers who want predictable, transparent delivery updates.

---

## 5. Solution and design decisions

| Decision | Rationale | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|
| Explain only **confirmed** stops | Avoids false claims when the system is only guessing | Feature stays silent when stop data is unavailable |
| Keep ETA prominent and show changes | ETA is the main source of anxiety | Frequent updates can feel unstable, so a change threshold is needed |
| Never show other customers' name, address, or exact location | Privacy and safety | Explanation stays generic |
| Plain-language copy | Works across literacy levels | Needs localization testing |
| Same layout as the current screen | Lower learning cost and lower build risk | Less room for bolder changes |

### Edge states to design

| State | Proposed behavior |
|---|---|
| No intermediate stop | Show the current screen with no sequence row |
| One stop | Show the sequence as designed |
| Two or more stops | Collapse to "2 nearby drop-offs first" |
| Stop cancelled mid-route | Remove it from the sequence and update the ETA with a reason |
| Large delay | Show a short apology line and a support option |
| Stop data unavailable | Fall back to the current screen. Never infer a stop from partner movement |

---

## 6. Competitor check

I will compare what each major app shows during live tracking, using my own screenshots from real orders.

| Element | App A | App B |
|---|---|---|
| Partner location on map | _to verify_ | _to verify_ |
| ETA shown and updated | _to verify_ | _to verify_ |
| Explains a detour or another stop | _to verify_ | _to verify_ |
| Status steps shown | _to verify_ | _to verify_ |

> Filled in only from what I observe directly. No claims about competitors without evidence.

---

## 7. Success measurement

| Metric | Role | Definition |
|---|---|---|
| **Delivery-status clarity score** | Primary | In-flow 1 to 5 rating: "How clear was your delivery status?" |
| Order-tracking satisfaction | Supporting | Rating after delivery |
| Delivery-related support contacts | Supporting | Route or ETA contacts per 1,000 orders |
| ETA accuracy | Guardrail | Mean absolute error between shown and actual arrival time |

**Decision rule (set before testing):** recommend a wider rollout only if clarity improves meaningfully over baseline **and** ETA accuracy does not get worse.

---

## 8. Validation plan

1. **Interview** five frequent customers about their last tracking experience.
2. **Prototype** the before and after screens at low fidelity.
3. **Test comprehension:** can users explain what is happening and why the ETA changed?
4. **Compare** results against a baseline measured on the current screen.
5. **Decide** on a wider rollout only after the above.

<details>
<summary>Interview guide</summary>

- Tell me about the last time you tracked an order. What did you look at first?
- Has the delivery partner ever taken a route that surprised you? What did you think was happening?
- What did you do next? (Wait, call, contact support?)
- What information would have helped most at that moment?
- After the prototype: in your own words, what is happening with this order?

</details>

**If validated, rollout:** A/B test on orders with confirmed stops, then expand, then iterate on copy, localization, and accessibility.

---

## 9. Limitations

- The problem comes from a **single personal observation**.
- **Prevalence** is unknown.
- **Cause** is unconfirmed. Confusion could come from batching, traffic, road closures, or something else.
- **Impact** of the proposed solution is unmeasured.
- The "before" screen is a recreation, not internal product data.
- Privacy handling for nearby drop-offs would need review by a privacy and legal team.

